WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Let’s deploy AOS Work on a single Linux server.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.567
Point your domains at the server.
Open ports 80 and 443.

00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:12.300
Using local MinIO? You’ll need a storage domain, too.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.967
Download deploy.sh from the
release linked in the guide.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:19.900
In Termius, upload the script
to this folder using SFTP.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:25.533
Open the server terminal and
type the command you see here.

00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:27.900
Press Enter to start the checks.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.433
The installer checks your processor,
memory, and deployment disk.

00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:36.500
Look at total capacity. You need a hundred gigabytes.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:40.700
Both ports say free. Nginx can use them on this host.

00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:44.900
Each domain gets its own HTTP and HTTPS check.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.400
This server still needs Docker, Nginx, and Certbot.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.700
Type y, then press Enter to install the missing tools.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:01:00.500
See “Environment ready”? Now
you can configure your domains.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.167
Choose one in the main menu
to set up domains and HTTPS.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.233
In the domain menu, choose one for Workbench.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.967
Enter your Workbench hostname. Then press Enter.

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:18.133
The installer checks DNS and configures encryption.

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:24.033
Wait for “settings saved” and the
full address before moving on.

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:28.933
Back in the domain menu, choose two for Studio.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.633
Enter your Studio hostname, then
press Enter to configure HTTPS.

00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:39.533
Next, choose three for Storage.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.967
For local object storage, enter
your Storage hostname here.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:46.433
Press Enter to continue.

00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:50.133
There it is. Your Storage settings are saved.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:54.700
With the three domains configured,
choose zero to go back.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.733
Choose two to see the available server packages.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.733
Your version may look different.
Choose one for the latest release.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.300
Need an older package? Follow
the Earlier releases link.

00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:12.600
“Server package ready” confirms the download.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.300
In the main menu, choose three to deploy AOS Work.

00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:22.567
Choose three again to install
both Studio and Workbench.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:25.700
Now let’s set up the middleware.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.800
For PostgreSQL, choose one to run it on this server.

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:35.567
Press Enter for the default
username, then set a password.

00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:41.567
The password stays hidden as you type.
Press Enter, then type it again to confirm.

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:44.367
Postgres and its vector extension are ready.

00:02:44.533 --> 00:02:49.400
For Redis, choose one to use the
local Docker Compose service.

00:02:49.633 --> 00:02:53.600
Give Redis its own password.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.800
For RabbitMQ, choose one to run it locally.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:02.900
Accept the default username
and set a RabbitMQ password.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:08.033
For object storage, choose
one to deploy MinIO locally.

00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:10.767
Set and confirm its password.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.033
Using hosted middleware? Choose External service.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:18.833
Once MinIO is ready, Studio
starts deploying automatically.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.733
Enter and confirm the password that
protects Studio’s encrypted data.

00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:29.133
Look for “Studio is running” and its
secure address. Workbench is next.

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:34.033
Set the default password for
new or reset Workbench users.

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:38.333
On a single host, enter zero for the sandbox host ID.

00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:43.833
Workbench passed its HTTP check.
Now let’s open both sites.

00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:49.133
Open the Studio address over HTTPS.
Look for the sign-in form.

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:54.433
Now open the secure Workbench address.
Its sign-in form should appear, too.

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:59.633
To update a service, choose four in the main menu.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:03.833
Find the service you want to update in this list.

00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:09.367
Here’s the guide’s example.
Studio Frontend is option two.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.633
Enter the Docker image tag you
want. Press Enter to update.

00:04:14.633 --> 00:04:19.667
Back in the recording, choose
zero to leave the update menu.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.333
Next, open the restart menu.

00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:27.433
Choose five in the main menu
to see your restart options.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:32.300
The first two restart groups. The
others restart individual services.

00:04:32.533 --> 00:04:37.300
For this walkthrough, choose
zero to leave everything running.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.333
Back at the main menu, check service health.

00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:45.667
Choose six and press Enter to see the health report.

00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:48.833
Give the full table a moment to appear.

00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:54.133
Look for “running.” Services with
health checks should also say “healthy.”

00:04:54.367 --> 00:04:58.467
The storage job runs once. Here, “exited” is expected.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:03.667
Twelve containers checked. All healthy.
Your deployment is complete.
